#!/usr/bin/env python3
r"""Per-session password gate in front of KasmVNC.

Why this exists
---------------
Not because KasmVNC fails to authenticate -- it does, properly. Measured against
the KasmVNC in this image (1.5.0):

    $ curl -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}' http://127.0.0.1:8082/          -> 401
    $ curl -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}' -u ncote:WRONG      .../        -> 401
    $ curl -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}' -u ncote:correct    .../        -> 200
    GET /websockify  without Authorization                                -> 401
    GET /websockify  with it, Origin: https://ondemand.ucar.edu           -> 101

with `WWW-Authenticate: Basic realm="Websockify"` on the refusals and
brute-force blacklisting behind it (-BlacklistThreshold, default 5 attempts).

The problem is the shape of that credential, not its strength. HTTP Basic is a
header the browser sends only after its own native dialog, so:

  * the dashboard's Connect button, which is a form POST, cannot supply it. The
    user has already authenticated to OnDemand; being asked to type a second
    password into a browser dialog is the thing this app exists not to do.
  * browsers cache Basic credentials per ORIGIN, and every session on this site
    shares the OnDemand origin. Two concurrent desktops would therefore fight
    over one cached credential, and the loser gets 401s it cannot explain.

So this process holds the public port, converts one POST from the dashboard into
a cookie, and adds the Authorization header itself on the way through to a
KasmVNC bound to loopback.

Design notes
------------
* KasmVNC's own auth stays ON behind this. That is affordable because every
  response it sends carries `Connection: close` and the server does close, even
  when the request asked for keep-alive -- verified by sending
  `Connection: keep-alive` and watching the socket end after one response. One
  request per connection means checking the first request head is checking every
  request, and adding the header to that one head covers all of them. A server
  that reused connections would need a full HTTP parser here, and would get
  -DisableBasicAuth instead.

* Any Authorization header the client sent is REMOVED before ours is added,
  never appended after. A browser that has a stale Basic credential cached for
  this origin -- the exact situation described above -- would otherwise send it,
  and KasmVNC reads the first one.

* Pure standard library. The relay costs nothing measurable: 64 MiB through this
  design moves at 2877 MiB/s (24 Gbit/s) on loopback against 10517 MiB/s
  direct, and a KasmVNC stream is tens of Mbit/s. The pixel path is not where
  the time goes.

* After the head, the connection is a blind byte relay in both directions. That
  is what keeps the desktop working at all: /websockify upgrades, and an
  upgraded connection is no longer HTTP to parse. A browser sends its cookies
  on the handshake like any other request, so the handshake is checked exactly
  like a GET.

* It injects one query parameter into the document request, and that is the
  only place the proxy prefix is handed to the client. See "The websocket path"
  below -- it is the difference between a desktop that paints and one that shows
  the client and then fails to connect.

* It never rewrites a body, and it deliberately does not put the proxy prefix
  into anything else the proxy handles itself. OnDemand's /rnode/ block does
  both halves:

      Header edit Location "^([^/]+//[^/]+)|(?=/)|^([\./]{1,}(?<!/))" \
                           "/rnode/%{MATCH_HOST}e/%{MATCH_PORT}e"
      Header edit* Set-Cookie ";\s*(?i)Path[^;]*" ""
      Header edit  Set-Cookie "^([^;]+)" "$1; Path=/rnode/%{MATCH_HOST}e/%{MATCH_PORT}e"

  -- it strips the prefix on the way in, and prepends it to every Location and
  rewrites every cookie Path on the way out. So the 302 this process sends after
  a successful sign-in has to be plain "/". Sent as /rnode/<node>/<port>/ it
  arrives at the browser as /rnode/<node>/<port>/rnode/<node>/<port>/, which is
  a 404 on the dashboard.

  What the proxy does NOT rewrite is response bodies. The login form's action is
  in a body, and the browser resolves it against the OnDemand host, so that one
  string does need the full prefix.

The websocket path
------------------
The client's own websocket URL is the one thing in KasmVNC that is NOT
prefix-agnostic, and it is worth being precise about because the symptom is
misleading: the page loads perfectly and then reports a failed connection.

Every asset reference in the client is relative, and `host`, `port` and
`encrypt` are all taken from window.location -- so all of those survive the
proxy. But the URL is assembled root-absolutely from a `path` setting whose
default is the bare string "websockify" (read out of the bundle in this image):

    a = o.getSetting("encrypt") ? "wss" : "ws";
    a += "://" + t;          // window.location.hostname
    r && (a += ":" + r);     // window.location.port, or 443/80
    a += "/" + i;            // o.getSetting("path"), default "websockify"

Behind /rnode/ that produces wss://ondemand.ucar.edu/websockify -- the
/rnode/<node>/<port> prefix is simply gone, and the handshake goes to the
OnDemand dashboard's own root instead of to this pod.

The settings resolver is what makes this fixable from here. From the same
bundle:

    function hr(n,e){ const t = IA(n); return t===null ? CA(n,e) : t }
    function Zb(n,e){ Mr[n] = e }

`hr` checks a URL query parameter FIRST and only then localStorage or the
default, and `Zb` -- what initSetting uses to record the resolved value -- writes
to an in-memory map, not to localStorage. So a `?path=` parameter wins over
everything and persists nothing: a stale value from an earlier session, which
would carry an earlier session's NodePort, cannot win over the one supplied
here.

So a GET for the client document with no `path=` of its own is answered with a
302 to the same document carrying `path=<prefix>websockify`. Root-relative and
unprefixed as always, so the proxy prepends the prefix to the Location. Done on
every document request rather than only after sign-in, so a reload gets the
right value too rather than reverting to the default.

CIRRUS_DESKTOP_ROOT_PATH therefore has two consumers: this, and the login form's
action.
"""

import base64
import hmac
import os
import re
import secrets
import socket
import sys
import threading
import urllib.parse

PROG = "cirrus-desktop-gate"

LISTEN_PORT = int(os.environ.get("CIRRUS_DESKTOP_PORT", "8080"))
UPSTREAM_PORT = int(os.environ.get("CIRRUS_DESKTOP_UPSTREAM_PORT", "8082"))
UPSTREAM_HOST = "127.0.0.1"
ROOT_PATH = os.environ.get("CIRRUS_DESKTOP_ROOT_PATH", "/")
PASSWORD_FILE = os.environ.get(
    "CIRRUS_DESKTOP_PASSWORD_FILE_RESOLVED", "/tmp/cirrus-desktop/session-password"
)
# The name KasmVNC knows, which is what kasmvncpasswd -u was given and what
# api/get_sessions reports back.
BASIC_USER = os.environ.get("CIRRUS_DESKTOP_BASIC_USER") or str(os.getuid())

COOKIE_NAME = "cirrus_desktop_gate"
SIGNIN_PATH = "cirrus-signin"

# The head of an HTTP request is small; anything larger than this before the
# blank line is not a request we should be forwarding.
MAX_HEAD = 64 * 1024
RELAY_CHUNK = 65536


def log(msg):
    print("%s: %s" % (PROG, msg), file=sys.stderr, flush=True)


def read_password():
    try:
        with open(PASSWORD_FILE) as fh:
            return fh.read().strip()
    except OSError as exc:
        log("cannot read %s: %s" % (PASSWORD_FILE, exc))
        return ""


# One token per process. It is what the cookie carries, so the password itself
# is never stored in the browser and a token from a previous pod is worthless.
SESSION_TOKEN = base64.urlsafe_b64encode(secrets.token_bytes(32)).decode().rstrip("=")


def http_response(status, body, extra_headers=()):
    body_bytes = body.encode("utf-8")
    head = [
        "HTTP/1.1 %s" % status,
        "Content-Type: text/html; charset=utf-8",
        "Content-Length: %d" % len(body_bytes),
        "Cache-Control: no-store",
        "X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff",
        "Connection: close",
    ]
    head.extend(extra_headers)
    return ("\r\n".join(head) + "\r\n\r\n").encode("utf-8") + body_bytes


LOGIN_PAGE = """<!doctype html>
<title>Ubuntu Desktop &middot; CIRRUS</title>
<style>
 body {{ font-family: system-ui, sans-serif; margin: 4rem auto; max-width: 30rem;
        color: #1a1a1a; background: #fff; }}
 form {{ display: flex; gap: .5rem; margin: 1.5rem 0; }}
 input[type=password] {{ flex: 1; padding: .5rem; font-size: 1rem; }}
 button {{ padding: .5rem 1rem; font-size: 1rem; }}
 p.err {{ color: #a00; }}
 p.note {{ color: #555; font-size: .9rem; }}
</style>
<h1>Ubuntu Desktop</h1>
{message}
<form method="post" action="{action}">
  <input type="password" name="password" autofocus placeholder="Session password">
  <button type="submit">Connect</button>
</form>
<p class="note">You should not normally see this page. This session has its own
password, generated when it launched, and the <b>Connect to Ubuntu Desktop</b>
button on the OnDemand dashboard sends it for you &mdash; go back there and use
it rather than typing anything here.</p>
"""


def login_page(action, error=None):
    message = '<p class="err">%s</p>' % error if error else ""
    return LOGIN_PAGE.format(message=message, action=action)


def parse_head(head):
    """Return (method, path, headers-dict-lowercased, lines). Malformed -> None."""
    try:
        text = head.decode("latin-1")
    except UnicodeDecodeError:
        return None
    lines = text.split("\r\n")
    parts = lines[0].split(" ")
    if len(parts) < 3:
        return None
    method, path = parts[0], parts[1]
    headers = {}
    for line in lines[1:]:
        if not line:
            continue
        name, _, value = line.partition(":")
        # Repeated headers are joined the way the RFC says to, which matters for
        # Cookie: browsers may send it more than once.
        name = name.strip().lower()
        value = value.strip()
        if name in headers:
            headers[name] = headers[name] + "; " + value
        else:
            headers[name] = value
    return method, path, headers, lines


def has_valid_cookie(headers):
    raw = headers.get("cookie", "")
    for item in raw.split(";"):
        name, _, value = item.strip().partition("=")
        if name == COOKIE_NAME:
            # compare_digest, not ==: the token is a secret and a timing
            # difference on a 43-character comparison is a real oracle.
            if hmac.compare_digest(value, SESSION_TOKEN):
                return True
    return False


def authorised_head(lines, password):
    """The request head to send upstream, carrying our Basic credential.

    Any Authorization the client sent is dropped rather than kept: a browser
    with a stale Basic credential cached for the OnDemand origin -- which is
    every browser that has ever been prompted by another session on this host --
    would otherwise supply it, and KasmVNC honours the first one it reads.
    """
    kept = [lines[0]]
    for line in lines[1:]:
        if not line:
            continue
        if line.split(":", 1)[0].strip().lower() == "authorization":
            continue
        kept.append(line)
    token = base64.b64encode(("%s:%s" % (BASIC_USER, password)).encode()).decode()
    kept.append("Authorization: Basic %s" % token)
    return "\r\n".join(kept).encode("latin-1")


def normalised_root():
    """The prefix the browser sees, with exactly one trailing slash.

    For the login form's action only. Never for a Location or a cookie Path --
    see the note in the module docstring about the proxy rewriting both.
    """
    root = ROOT_PATH if ROOT_PATH.startswith("/") else "/" + ROOT_PATH
    return root if root.endswith("/") else root + "/"


def signin_target(raw):
    """Where to send the browser after a correct password, as the APP sees it.

    Root-relative and unprefixed, always: the proxy prepends its prefix to
    whatever Location it forwards. Talking to this gate directly -- the
    acceptance test, a port-forward -- "/" is also exactly right.

    A caller-supplied `to` is honoured only if it is a single-slash-rooted path.
    "//host/..." and "/\\host/..." are how a browser is sent to another origin,
    and CR or LF would be a header injection, so all three are refused rather
    than sanitised.
    """
    if (
        raw.startswith("/")
        and not raw.startswith("//")
        and not raw.startswith("/\\")
        and "\r" not in raw
        and "\n" not in raw
    ):
        return raw
    return "/"


SIGNIN_URL = normalised_root() + SIGNIN_PATH

# The documents that load the VNC client, and therefore the ones that need the
# websocket path handed to them. screen.html is the secondary-monitor window,
# which the client opens itself with a pathname-derived URL -- correctly
# prefixed, but with no `path` of its own, so it needs the same treatment.
CLIENT_DOCUMENTS = ("/", "/index.html", "/vnc.html", "/screen.html")


def websocket_path():
    """The `path` setting the client needs, relative to the websocket root.

    No leading slash: the client builds its URL as scheme://host[:port] + "/" +
    path, so a leading slash here produces a doubled one.
    """
    return normalised_root().lstrip("/") + "websockify"


def needs_path_param(method, target):
    """Should this request be answered with a redirect that adds `path`?"""
    if method != "GET":
        return False
    path, _, query = target.partition("?")
    if path not in CLIENT_DOCUMENTS:
        return False
    # Nothing to fix when the app really is at the root -- a direct connection,
    # a port-forward, the acceptance test. "websockify" is already the default.
    if websocket_path() == "websockify":
        return False
    # Already carries one, whoever put it there. Redirecting again would loop.
    return "path" not in urllib.parse.parse_qs(query, keep_blank_values=True)


def with_path_param(target):
    path, _, query = target.partition("?")
    # safe="/" so the slashes in the prefix stay readable in the URL bar rather
    # than becoming %2F, which is also what the client expects to read back.
    addition = "path=" + urllib.parse.quote(websocket_path(), safe="/")
    query = (query + "&" + addition) if query else addition
    return path + "?" + query


def redirect_response(location):
    return http_response(
        "302 Found",
        '<a href="%s">Ubuntu Desktop</a>' % location,
        ("Location: %s" % location,),
    )


def is_signin_request(path):
    """Does this request target the sign-in endpoint?

    Matched on the suffix, not the whole path, because the two OnDemand proxies
    deliver it differently: /rnode/ strips its prefix, so the request arrives as
    /cirrus-signin, while a direct connection or a non-stripping proxy would
    deliver the whole /rnode/<node>/<port>/cirrus-signin.
    """
    return path.split("?", 1)[0].endswith("/" + SIGNIN_PATH)


def read_head(sock):
    """Read up to and including the blank line. Returns (head, leftover)."""
    buf = b""
    while b"\r\n\r\n" not in buf:
        if len(buf) > MAX_HEAD:
            return None, b""
        try:
            chunk = sock.recv(RELAY_CHUNK)
        except OSError:
            return None, b""
        if not chunk:
            return None, b""
        buf += chunk
    head, _, rest = buf.partition(b"\r\n\r\n")
    return head, rest


def read_body(sock, headers, already):
    length = headers.get("content-length", "")
    if not re.fullmatch(r"\d{1,9}", length):
        return already
    length = int(length)
    body = already
    while len(body) < length:
        try:
            chunk = sock.recv(min(RELAY_CHUNK, length - len(body)))
        except OSError:
            break
        if not chunk:
            break
        body += chunk
    return body


def relay(src, dst):
    try:
        while True:
            data = src.recv(RELAY_CHUNK)
            if not data:
                break
            dst.sendall(data)
    except OSError:
        pass
    finally:
        # Half-close rather than close: the other direction may still be
        # delivering, which for a websocket is the normal state of affairs.
        for sock in (src, dst):
            try:
                sock.shutdown(socket.SHUT_RDWR)
            except OSError:
                pass


def proxy(client, head, leftover):
    upstream = socket.create_connection((UPSTREAM_HOST, UPSTREAM_PORT), timeout=30)
    upstream.settimeout(None)
    client.settimeout(None)
    upstream.sendall(head + b"\r\n\r\n" + leftover)
    threads = [
        threading.Thread(target=relay, args=(client, upstream), daemon=True),
        threading.Thread(target=relay, args=(upstream, client), daemon=True),
    ]
    for thread in threads:
        thread.start()
    for thread in threads:
        thread.join()
    for sock in (client, upstream):
        try:
            sock.close()
        except OSError:
            pass


def handle(client, addr):
    try:
        client.settimeout(60)
        head, leftover = read_head(client)
        if head is None:
            return
        parsed = parse_head(head)
        if parsed is None:
            client.sendall(http_response("400 Bad Request", "<h1>Bad request</h1>"))
            return
        method, path, headers, lines = parsed
        # The form action has to be what the BROWSER sees, which is not what
        # this process sees: OnDemand's /rnode/ proxy strips its prefix before
        # forwarding, so the request path here has no prefix at all, and a body
        # is the one thing the proxy will not rewrite on the way back. The
        # prefix is passed in instead, computed by an init container from the
        # node name and the assigned NodePort -- neither of which exists until
        # the pod is scheduled.
        action = SIGNIN_URL

        if has_valid_cookie(headers):
            # The client document needs to be told the proxy prefix, because the
            # one URL it does not build relatively is the websocket. See "The
            # websocket path" in the module docstring.
            if needs_path_param(method, path):
                client.sendall(redirect_response(with_path_param(path)))
                return
            password = read_password()
            if not password:
                # The password file has gone. Refusing is the only honest
                # answer: relaying without the header would hand the browser
                # KasmVNC's own 401 and its native dialog, which is precisely
                # the experience this gate exists to prevent.
                client.sendall(
                    http_response(
                        "503 Service Unavailable",
                        "<h1>This session has lost its password file</h1>",
                    )
                )
                return
            proxy(client, authorised_head(lines, password), leftover)
            return

        if method == "POST" and is_signin_request(path):
            body = read_body(client, headers, leftover)
            fields = urllib.parse.parse_qs(body.decode("latin-1", "replace"))
            supplied = (fields.get("password") or [""])[0]
            expected = read_password()
            if expected and hmac.compare_digest(supplied, expected):
                # Both of these are what the APP is mounted at, not what the
                # browser sees. The proxy rewrites the Location and replaces the
                # cookie's Path with /rnode/<node>/<port>, which is what scopes
                # the cookie to this session and to nothing else on the OnDemand
                # host; setting that prefix here instead gets it applied twice.
                redirect = signin_target((fields.get("to") or [""])[0])
                # Carry the websocket path straight away, so the ordinary
                # Connect-button flow does not need the extra redirect that a
                # bare reload would take.
                if needs_path_param("GET", redirect):
                    redirect = with_path_param(redirect)
                cookie_path = "/"
                log("sign-in accepted from %s" % (addr[0],))
                client.sendall(
                    http_response(
                        "302 Found",
                        '<a href="%s">Ubuntu Desktop</a>' % redirect,
                        (
                            "Location: %s" % redirect,
                            "Set-Cookie: %s=%s; Path=%s; HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax"
                            % (COOKIE_NAME, SESSION_TOKEN, cookie_path),
                        ),
                    )
                )
            else:
                log("sign-in refused from %s" % (addr[0],))
                client.sendall(
                    http_response(
                        "401 Unauthorized",
                        login_page(action, "That is not this session's password."),
                    )
                )
            return

        # Anything else without a cookie gets the login form, with the status
        # matching what it is: a refusal for an API call, a page for a browser.
        # Deliberately no WWW-Authenticate header -- that is what would summon
        # the browser's own credential dialog.
        status = "200 OK" if method == "GET" else "401 Unauthorized"
        client.sendall(http_response(status, login_page(action)))
    except OSError:
        pass
    finally:
        try:
            client.close()
        except OSError:
            pass


def main():
    password = read_password()
    if not password:
        log("no session password is available; refusing to start.")
        log("Every request would be rejected, which is worse than not starting:")
        log("the pod would look healthy and no one could get in.")
        return 1

    listener = socket.socket(socket.AF_INET, socket.SOCK_STREAM)
    listener.setsockopt(socket.SOL_SOCKET, socket.SO_REUSEADDR, 1)
    listener.bind(("0.0.0.0", LISTEN_PORT))
    listener.listen(128)
    log(
        "listening on 0.0.0.0:%d, forwarding to %s:%d as '%s' (sign-in at %s)"
        % (LISTEN_PORT, UPSTREAM_HOST, UPSTREAM_PORT, BASIC_USER, SIGNIN_URL)
    )

    while True:
        try:
            client, addr = listener.accept()
        except OSError as exc:
            log("accept failed: %s" % exc)
            continue
        threading.Thread(target=handle, args=(client, addr), daemon=True).start()


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
